From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bert Belder <bertbelder@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: Per-IsolateGroup page allocator for out-of-sandbox memory

An isolate group keeps two regions outside of its sandbox and pointer
compression cage: the code range and, with the sandbox, the trusted
range. Both are reserved from the process-wide platform page allocator
at addresses V8 picks, even when the embedder placed the group's sandbox
in its own reservation (v8::IsolateGroup::CreateParams). Embedders that
manage the address space themselves, for example to apply hardware
memory isolation per isolate group, need those regions to be placed
too.

Add v8::IsolateGroup::CreateParams::page_allocator. When set, the
group's trusted range and code range are reserved through it instead of
the platform page allocator: TrustedRange::InitReservation() takes the
allocator from the group, and CodeRange::InitReservation() takes an
optional out-of-cage allocator that replaces the platform allocator it
otherwise switches to for the external code space. Everything else
about the two ranges (alignment, hints, permissions, red zones, the
BoundedPageAllocator on top) is unchanged, and a null allocator keeps
the existing behavior.

A CppHeap has its own page allocator, taken from the platform. Add
v8::CppHeapCreateParams::page_allocator so an embedder that creates the
CppHeap itself can allocate its pages through the same per-group
allocator; when null the platform's allocator (or cppgc's default) is
used as before.

